  "subject": "🧠 Axios Finish Line: Power of art",
  "date": "Tue, 20 Oct 2026 21:29:21 -0400",
  "body_text": "96 \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n 🌀 Plus: Outer Banks bluster | Tuesday, October 20, 2026 \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n View in browser \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n PRESENTED BY PEPSICO \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n Axios Finish Line \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n By Mike Allen and Erica Pandey and Jim VandeHei &middot; Oct 20, 2026 \r\n \r\n \r\n 🌙 Good evening! Smart Brevity™ count: 341 words … 1½ mins. Edited by Natalie Daher and copy edited by Amy Stern. \n\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n 1 big thing: The art of connection \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n A ReCollections session at the museum. Photo: Courtesy of the San Antonio Museum of Art The paintings and sculptures at the San Antonio Museum of Art do more than inspire creativity. They're helping people living with dementia connect with others.\n\n Why it matters: Alzheimer's and other forms of dementia can make social connection increasingly difficult. But art can offer a way to communicate, share stories and build relationships. 🎨 How it works: The museum's free ReCollections program brings together people living with dementia and their caregivers to explore art, Axios San Antonio's Madalyn Mendoza reports. \n\n Participants discuss works from the museum's collection, using them as prompts for memories and personal stories. They then make their own art and share a meal together. 💬 \"The artworks are kind of a touchstone, but we're really looking to explore personal experience,\" museum education director Tripp Cardiff tells Axios.\n\n A gallery conversation during a ReCollections event. Photo: Courtesy of the San Antonio Museum of Art 🖼️ The big picture: What began as a virtual program during the pandemic has grown into regular in-person gatherings, drawing about 477 participants in the museum's last fiscal year.\n\n Cardiff says he's watched participants form friendships and even make plans to see one another outside the museum. The museum is beginning to collect formal feedback on the program's impact. Between the lines: ReCollections also extends beyond the scheduled sessions. \n\n The free program offers three sessions each quarter — one at UT Health's Center for Brain Health and two at the museum. 🖌️ The museum also supplies hundreds of art kits to the Center for Brain Health, where people can make art on their own or take a kit home. The bottom line: Sometimes a work of art is less about what you see than the conversation it starts.\n\n For Texan readers: Upcoming sessions and registration information are available through Eventbrite . Share this story ... Get Axios Local : Newsletters in 43 cities. \n\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\nA MESSAGE FROM PEPSICO\r\n\n \r\n AI is changing the decision process \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n PepsiCo uses AI to connect data across the enterprise, helping teams move from hindsight to foresight and make faster decisions.\n\n\r\n Key numbers: 300,000 associates making three better decisions a day can create nearly a million better decisions daily.\n\n\r\n See the story. \n\n\r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n 2. 🌾 Parting shot: Windswept wonder \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n Photo: Marcia Kneisel Reader Marcia Kneisel of Littleton, N.C., captured this morning scene along North Carolina's Outer Banks — in the town of Kill Devil Hills during a brief fall getaway. \n\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n 🏁 Please invite your friends to join Finish Line . \n\n \r\n 👆 Like this comms style and format? It's called Smart Brevity®. Bring it to your org — via hands-on training or internal comms software — to harness its power and impact. \r\n &nbsp; Axios thanks our partners for supporting our journalism.\n Advertise with us. \n\n Read more about Axios' mission , ethics and how we use AI in our journalism . You can reach the authors by replying to this email.\nSponsorship has no influence on editorial content.\n\n Thank you for signing up for this Axios newsletter. To stop receiving this newsletter, unsubscribe or manage your email preferences . If someone forwarded this to you, sign up now to get Axios in your inbox.\n\n Axios, PO Box 101060, Arlington VA 22201 &nbsp; Follow Axios across:\n\n &nbsp; &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;",
