/em></p></td>\r\n</tr><tr>\r\n<td height=\"10\" style=\"height:10px;font-size:0;border-bottom:10px solid #f5f5f5\">&nbsp;</td>\r\n</tr>      <tr>\r\n<td height=\"10\" style=\"height:10px;font-size:0\">&nbsp;</td>\r\n</tr><tr>\r\n<td style=\"padding:20px 3.5% 10px;line-height:1.5\">\r\n<span class=\"b k\" style=\"font-size:27px;font-family:'gorditamedium',Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;color:#222;letter-spacing:-0.1px;line-height:1.5\">  1 big thing: The art of connection </span>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>   <tr>\r\n<td height=\"10\" style=\"height:10px;font-size:0\">&nbsp;</td>\r\n</tr> <tr>\r\n<td class=\"v\" style=\"padding:0 3.5%;line-height:1.75;font-size:17px;font-family:Georgia,serif;color:#222;letter-spacing:-0.1px\"><div align=\"center\"><!--[if mso]><img alt='' src='https://images.axios.com/HWFvKsY0OWSEV1BsYTCV4ooIBto=/fit-in/1116x1300/2026/10/20/1792508636568.jpg' style='display:block; margin: 0 auto; max-width:100%;' width='558' /><![endif]--><!--[if !mso]><!--><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://images.axios.com/HWFvKsY0OWSEV1BsYTCV4ooIBto=/fit-in/1116x1300/2026/10/20/1792508636568.jpg\" style=\"display:block; margin: 0 auto; max-width:100%;\" width=\"100%\"/><!--<![endif]--></div> <div class=\"bodytext\" style=\"font-size:13px; font-family:'Gordita',Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif; color:#848484; margin-bottom:20px;\">A ReCollections session at the museum. Photo: Courtesy of the San Antonio Museum of Art</div><p><strong>The paintings and sculptures</strong> at the San Antonio Museum of Art do more than inspire creativity. They're helping people living with dementia connect with others.</p><ul><li><strong>Why it matters:</strong> Alzheimer's and other forms of dementia can make social connection increasingly difficult. But art can offer a way to communicate, share stories and build relationships.</li></ul><p><strong>🎨 How it works:</strong> The museum's free <a href=\"https://link.axios.com/click/48021312.367820/aHR0cHM6Ly93d3cuYXhpb3MuY29tL2xvY2FsL3Nhbi1hbnRvbmlvLzIwMjYvMTAvMDYvc2FtYS1yZWNvbGxlY3Rpb25zLWFydC1hbHpoZWltZXJzLWRlbWVudGlhP3V0bV9zb3VyY2U9bmV3c2xldHRlciZ1dG1fbWVkaXVtPWVtYWlsJnV0bV9jYW1wYWlnbj1uZXdzbGV0dGVyX2F4aW9zZmluaXNobGluZSZzdHJlYW09dG9w/6a14a774a4e0095e0a03c94aBf2cf1896\" target=\"_blank\">ReCollections program</a> brings together people living with dementia and their caregivers to explore art, <em>Axios San Antonio's Madalyn Mendoza reports.</em></p><ul><li><strong>Participants discuss works</strong> from the museum's collection, using them as prompts for memories and personal stories.</li><li><strong>They then make</strong> their own art and share a meal together.</li></ul><p>💬 <strong>\"The artworks are </strong>kind of a touchstone, but we're really looking to explore personal experience,\" museum education director Tripp Cardiff tells Axios.</p><div align=\"center\"><!--[if mso]><img alt='' src='https://images.axios.com/x_CNrbtDupM6fIzwXLo-hOcS05A=/fit-in/1116x1300/2026/10/20/1792508486111.jpg' style='display:block; margin: 0 auto; max-width:100%;' width='558' /><![endif]--><!--[if !mso]><!--><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://images.axios.com/x_CNrbtDupM6fIzwXLo-hOcS05A=/fit-in/1116x1300/2026/10/20/1792508486111.jpg\" style=\"display:block; margin: 0 auto; max-width:100%;\" width=\"100%\"/><!--<![endif]--></div> <div class=\"bodytext\" style=\"font-size:13px; font-family:'Gordita',Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif; color:#848484; margin-bottom:20px;\">A gallery conversation during a ReCollections event. Photo: Courtesy of the San Antonio Museum of Art</div><p><strong>🖼️ The big picture:</strong> What began as a virtual program during the pandemic has grown into regular in-person gatherings, drawing about 477 participants in the museum's last fiscal year.</p><ul><li><strong>Cardiff says </strong>he's watched participants form friendships and even make plans to see one another outside the museum.</li><li><strong>The museum</strong> is beginning to collect formal feedback on the program's impact.</li></ul><p><strong>Between the lines:</strong> ReCollections also extends beyond the scheduled sessions. </p><ul><li><strong>The free program</strong> offers three sessions each quarter — one at UT Health's Center for Brain Health and two at the museum.</li><li>🖌️ <strong> The museum also supplies </strong>hundreds of art kits to the Center for Brain Health, where people can make art on their own or take a kit home.</li></ul><p><strong>The bottom line:</strong> Sometimes a work of art is less about what you see than the conversation it starts.</p><ul><li><strong>For Texan readers:</strong> Upcoming sessions and registration information are available through <a href=\"https://link.axios.com/click/48021312.367820/aHR0cHM6Ly93d3cuZXZlbnRicml0ZS5jb20vZS9yZWNvbGxlY3Rpb25zLWF0LXRoZS1zYW1hLWFydC1jb252ZXJzYXRpb25zLXRvLXN0aW11bGF0ZS10aGUtbWluZC10aWNrZXRzLTIwMDIzMDQ3OTM1Mzg_YWZmPWVyZWxwYW5lbG9yZyZ1dG1fc291cmNlPW5ld3NsZXR0ZXImdXRtX21lZGl1bT1lbWFpbCZ1dG1fY2FtcGFpZ249bmV3c2xldHRlcl9heGlvc2ZpbmlzaGxpbmUmc3RyZWFtPXRvcA/6a14a774a4e0095e0a03c94aB284e6298\" target=\"_blank\">Eventbrite</a>.</li></ul><p><em><a href=\"https://link.axios.com/click/48021312.367820/aHR0cHM6Ly93d3cuYXhpb3MuY29tL2xvY2FsL3Nhbi1hbnRvbmlvLzIwMjYvMTAvMDYvc2FtYS1yZWNvbGxlY3Rpb25zLWFydC1hbHpoZWltZXJzLWRlbWVudGlhP3V0bV9zb3VyY2U9bmV3c2xldHRlciZ1dG1fbWVkaXVtPWVtYWlsJnV0bV9jYW1wYWlnbj1uZXdzbGV0dGVyX2F4aW9zZmluaXNobGluZSZzdHJlYW09dG9w/6a14a774a4e0095e0a03c94aCf2cf1896\" target=\"_blank\">Share this story</a> ... <a href=\"https://link.axios.com/click/48021312.367820/aHR0cHM6Ly93d3cuYXhpb3MuY29tL25ld3NsZXR0ZXJzL2xvY2FsP3V0bV9zb3VyY2U9bmV3c2xldHRlciZ1dG1fbWVkaXVtPWVtYWlsJnV0bV9jYW1wYWlnbj1uZXdzbGV0dGVyX2F4aW9zZmluaXNobGluZSZzdHJlYW09dG9w/6a14a774a4e0095e0a03c94aB4c0993d2\" target=\"_blank\">Get Axios Local</a>: Newsletters in 43 cities.</em></p></td>\r\n</tr> <tr><td align=\"center\" style=\"padding:20px 3.5% 25px\">   <a href=\"https://link.axios.com/click/48021312.367820/aHR0cHM6Ly93d3cuZmFjZWJvb2suY29tL3NoYXJlci5waHA_dT1odHRwczovL3d3dy5heGlvcy5jb20vbmV3c